<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Saramaka</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Saramaccan, Saramaka people</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Saramaka are a maroon people of central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suriname">Suriname</a>, descended from Africans who escaped from plantations in the late 17th and 18th centuries and settled along the upper <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suriname-river">Suriname River</a>. After decades of war they won a peace treaty with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> colony in 1762. They keep their own political institutions and language, Saramaccan, and won a landmark land-rights ruling at the Inter-American Court in 2007.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/saramaka.jpg" alt="Saramaka" width="320" height="570" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Johnston, Harry Hamilton, Sir, 1858-1927 · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Saramaka-1910.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The ancestors of the Saramaka were Africans of many peoples, among them the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akan">Akan</a>, Bakongo and Fon, sold to plantations in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suriname">Suriname</a>, who escaped into the rainforest alone, in small groups and in great collective rebellions, and fought there for their independence for nearly a hundred years. Their language, Saramaccan, which they share with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/matawai">Matawai</a>, is a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/criollo-people">creole</a> related to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sranan-tongo">Sranan Tongo</a>: about half its words come from West and Central African languages, a fifth each from English and Portuguese, and the rest from Amerindian languages and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>After their treaty the Saramaka lived almost as a state within a state until the mid-20th century. From the 1960s about 6,000 of them were moved to transmigration villages on the Lower Suriname River when around half their territory was flooded for a hydroelectric project supplying an aluminium smelter. The civil war of the late 1980s between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maroons">Maroons</a> and the military government brought great hardship, and by mid-1989 about 3,000 Saramaka were refugees in French Guiana, where an accord of 1883 between the governor and the Saramaka Granman already allowed them to live.</p><p>After the war the government granted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timber">timber</a> and mining concessions in Saramaka territory to foreign companies without consulting the Saramaka authorities. The Association of Saramaka Authorities complained to the Inter-American Commission of Human Rights in the mid-1990s, and in November 2007 the Inter-American Court for Human Rights granted the Saramaka collective rights to the lands of their ancestors, a say over timber and gold, and compensation for earlier timber grants, paid into a development fund that they manage.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>Saramaka society rests on matrilineal clans, lo, each descended from an original band of escaped slaves and holding land claimed in the early 18th century, and on lineages, bee, of 50 to 150 people. Villages of 100 to 200 people stand by rivers. Women do most of the farming of hill <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cassava">cassava</a> and other crops once men have cleared the fields, while men hunt, fish and spend years working for wages on the coast.</p><p>A paramount chief, the gaamá, headmen, kabiteni, and assistant headmen, basiá, hold offices that belong to the clans. Problems are settled in council meetings, kuútu, and through divination, by which village deities, ancestors, forest spirits and snake gods are consulted in every part of life. About a quarter of the Saramaka are nominal Christians, mainly Moravian.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Saramaka” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Many enslaved people escaped into the rainforest and formed maroon societies, among them the Ndyuka, <b>Saramaka</b> and Matawai, whose descendants still live in the interior.</blockquote><a href="/empire/suriname">Suriname · Socioeconomy</a></li></ul></section>
